00:02:31.585 --> 00:02:33.210
and imagine that
you're trying to build

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:35.580
a mobile app for your company.

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:37.830
If you're starting a
project from scratch

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:39.480
and you want to
build it yourself,

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:42.400
you're likely going to have to
think about a couple of things.

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:45.180
Number one, setting
up a database,

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:47.790
which you'll probably have
to run and scale yourself

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:50.550
if you're taking
something off the shelf.

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:54.150
In front of that, you'll need
to put some sort of API layer

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:57.150
that can make queries
from your clients.

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:00.540
That probably means also
adding an authentication layer

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:03.840
so that you can make sure
that users that are accessing

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.740
your data are authenticated.

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:09.790
And this also means adding
some sort of sync logic

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:12.290
so that you can make sure you
make sure your data is syncing

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:14.300
with the local results.

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:17.227
And finally, these local
results are probably

00:03:17.227 --> 00:03:19.310
going to be stored in a
completely separate client

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:21.350
database that again,
you have to make

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:23.420
sure is syncing
with your database

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:25.290
that we mentioned
up at the front.

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:27.350
Now when you arrive
here, all of a sudden you

00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:30.650
have a pretty complex
set of solutions.

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:32.990
Each of these as a
standalone could actually

00:03:32.990 --> 00:03:34.620
be a whole company in itself.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:36.195
And that's what we
actually see today

00:03:36.195 --> 00:03:38.195
is that people are having
to go to many vendors,

00:03:38.195 --> 00:03:41.480
many different places, to
actually accomplish this stack

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:44.180
to build their
mobile or web apps.

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:47.060
And this is why we
built Firestore.

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:49.130
Firestore was built to
tackle these problems that

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:51.900
are very common to mobile
and web app development

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:55.130
and to simplify this
process for you.

00:03:55.130 --> 00:03:58.140
For those that are familiar
with Cloud Datastore

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:00.230
and the Firebase
Realtime Database,

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:04.040
an important question is, how
does this relate to those?

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:06.500
Cloud Firestore is
the next generation

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:08.330
of cloud data storage.

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:10.310
And it includes
important learnings

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:13.670
from the Firebase
Realtime Database.

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:15.620
So we took a look at
the developer wishlists

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:18.769
from each of these,
and we used those

00:04:18.769 --> 00:04:20.269
to inform the
decisions that we made

00:04:20.269 --> 00:04:22.343
when built in Cloud Firestore.

00:04:22.343 --> 00:04:24.510
So you can think of this
as the best of both worlds.

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:26.420
It takes some of the best
elements of Datastore

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:28.212
and some of the best
elements and learnings

00:04:28.212 --> 00:04:31.310
from Firebase Realtime Database.

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:35.360
The first thing to know
is that Firestore is fully

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:38.600
supported in the Firebase SDKs.

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:41.720
This includes web,
iOS, and Android,

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:45.340
so you can access data
directly from the clients.

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:48.040
This is actually pretty
unusual for a database,

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:52.600
and it's not lost on us that
letting clients talk directly

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:56.020
to your database can pose
all sorts of challenges.

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:57.700
For one, you can't
always trust the data

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:00.180
that's coming from clients.

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:02.650
And secondly, you're dealing
with potentially unauthorized

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:03.740
users.

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:07.840
So this is where Firebase
Auth and security rules

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:10.270
come in handy.

00:05:10.270 --> 00:05:13.570
These allow you to validate
operations from the client,

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:16.480
and this removes the need to
build that middle tier that you

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:18.670
saw on the previous
slide, which can sometimes

00:05:18.670 --> 00:05:21.604
be its own standalone server
just to manage authentication.

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:26.150
Now the next thing
that's very important

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:29.690
is the ability to have
these applications

00:05:29.690 --> 00:05:31.520
work across multiple
devices when we're

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:33.620
talking about client access.

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:35.490
Maybe you put down your
phone, you walk over

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:37.430
to your desktop
computer, and you

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:40.130
expect to resume the
same state in your app

00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:41.550
that you're operating on.

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:43.882
So this is something that
Firestore handles natively,

00:05:43.882 --> 00:05:46.340
and it makes it really, really
great for those applications

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:48.050
that you want to
develop and serve

00:05:48.050 --> 00:05:52.630
to multiple types of
devices at one time.

00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:54.990
Now another problem
that's very, very common

00:05:54.990 --> 00:06:00.660
these days is people expect
their apps to work offline.

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:02.400
It's fairly normal
for people to look

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:04.440
at their mobile
applications when

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:06.602
there's little to no
chance of connectivity.

00:06:06.602 --> 00:06:08.310
Maybe you're running
through the airport,

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:10.290
you're at the top of
a mountain, or maybe

00:06:10.290 --> 00:06:13.120
you've gone offline on purpose.

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:15.990
So the same
technology that allows

00:06:15.990 --> 00:06:18.870
us to operate in offline mode
also helps in the situations

00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:21.600
where we have spotty
network connection.

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:25.290
And so as you can imagine, this
is very challenging to solve.

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:28.210
And we've actually built
this natively into Firestore.

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:31.350
It requires building complex
sync and conflict resolution

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:34.240
logic on the client.

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:36.810
So in Cloud Firestore,
our client libraries

00:06:36.810 --> 00:06:39.495
come fully equipped to
handle these use cases.

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:45.780
So let's talk about the
data model in Firestore.

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:49.560
We've mentioned already that
Firestore is a document store.

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:52.620
So at the smallest
unit, the smallest unit

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:55.070
of storage in Firestore
is a document.

00:06:55.070 --> 00:06:57.630
And a document, as you can
see up here on the screen,

00:06:57.630 --> 00:07:01.680
is a lightweight record that
contains key value pairs.

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:04.410
There is no schema
enforcement in Firestore,

00:07:04.410 --> 00:07:06.810
so you have complete
freedom over what

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:10.470
fields you put in a document.

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:15.110
Now documents can be grouped
together in related groups

00:07:15.110 --> 00:07:17.680
that we call collections.

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:20.470
And you can see that
representation up here.

00:07:20.470 --> 00:07:23.810
Documents can also map
to sub-collections,

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:27.040
and this lends itself really
nicely to hierarchical data

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:31.310
that's very common in web
and mobile app development.

00:07:31.310 --> 00:07:34.360
So what we've seen so
far is that this model

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:36.790
works really, really
well for developers,

00:07:36.790 --> 00:07:41.440
and is designed really
well to scale as well.

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:45.570
So let's talk about what's
new in Cloud Firestore.

00:07:45.570 --> 00:07:47.520
As we mentioned,
called Firestore

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:49.740
went generally available
just earlier this year

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:51.070
at the end of January.

00:07:51.070 --> 00:07:53.640
It was in beta for a little
over a year before that--

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:55.440
since 2017.

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:58.260
So as part of the announcement
of going generally available,

00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:02.130
we have some pretty cool new
things that we've announced.

00:08:02.130 --> 00:08:03.630
So the first thing
I want to mention

00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:08.100
is that Cloud Firestore
operates in two different modes,

00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:11.140
Datastore Mode and Native Mode.

00:08:11.140 --> 00:08:13.480
These have slightly
different strengths

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:15.430
that I will highlight
based on the use case

00:08:15.430 --> 00:08:18.030
that you're looking for.

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:21.380
So the first thing is
that Datastore Mode

00:08:21.380 --> 00:08:25.820
has the exact same API and
same scale as Datastore,

00:08:25.820 --> 00:08:28.440
but a new storage layer.

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:30.170
So if you are a
Datastore customer,

00:08:30.170 --> 00:08:32.580
this will feel very
familiar to you.

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:36.409
And in fact, eventually, all
existing Datastore customers

00:08:36.409 --> 00:08:40.880
will be able to upgrade to
Firestore and Datastore mode.

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:43.549
Datastore mode also
has some new things

00:08:43.549 --> 00:08:46.250
that are greater than
some of the things offered

00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:47.570
in Datastore today.

00:08:47.570 --> 00:08:50.090
And those things include
strong consistency,

00:08:50.090 --> 00:08:52.670
where we had eventually
consistent queries before

00:08:52.670 --> 00:08:53.810
in Datastore.

00:08:53.810 --> 00:08:57.160
We also have a higher
availability SLA, which

00:08:57.160 --> 00:08:58.690
I'll talk about in a second.

00:08:58.690 --> 00:09:01.480
And we also have a new
data flow connector.

00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:03.730
So on the other side of
things, we have Native Mode.

00:09:03.730 --> 00:09:07.480
And Native Mode leverages
this new document model,

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:09.875
and it integrates with
third party clients.

00:09:09.875 --> 00:09:11.500
And that's kind of
the main difference.

00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:15.250
And for those of you coming from
the Firebase Realtime Database

00:09:15.250 --> 00:09:17.230
world, it's more
akin to the model

00:09:17.230 --> 00:09:20.320
there with the
direct line access.

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:21.820
A simple way to
think about this is

00:09:21.820 --> 00:09:25.190
that Datastore Mode is
optimized and scales really,

00:09:25.190 --> 00:09:29.192
really well for your rights,
and Native Mode scales

00:09:29.192 --> 00:09:31.150
really, really well for
concurrent connections.

00:09:31.150 --> 00:09:33.220
So when you're thinking about
all these different clients

00:09:33.220 --> 00:09:35.303
trying to connect to the
same application at once.

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:39.800
As part of going
generally available,

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:42.570
we also announced
10 new regions--

00:09:42.570 --> 00:09:45.130
10 new locations that
are a combination

00:09:45.130 --> 00:09:47.560
of multi-region and regional.

00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:49.780
So you can see we have
two new regional--

00:09:49.780 --> 00:09:52.150
sorry, two new
multi-regional locations

00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:54.100
that are highlighted in green.

00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:56.840
We have one in the
US and one in Europe.

00:09:56.840 --> 00:09:59.140
And these multi-region
locations can actually

00:09:59.140 --> 00:10:01.300
withstand the loss
of entire regions

00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:05.410
because they're replicated
across more than one.

00:10:05.410 --> 00:10:08.710
And this maintains availability
without losing data,

00:10:08.710 --> 00:10:10.810
and all of these regions
are actually required

00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:14.480
to be at least 100 miles apart.

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:17.480
In addition, we have new
service level agreements

00:10:17.480 --> 00:10:19.940
that are attached to each
of these different types

00:10:19.940 --> 00:10:21.690
of locations.

00:10:21.690 --> 00:10:25.070
So the first for multi-region,
which is our highest SLA--

00:10:25.070 --> 00:10:27.140
we have five nines availability.

00:10:27.140 --> 00:10:30.782
And for regional, we
have four nines SLA.

00:10:30.782 --> 00:10:32.990
Both of these are actually
improvements over the SLAs

00:10:32.990 --> 00:10:36.770
that you saw in Datastore, which
were four nines and three nines

00:10:36.770 --> 00:10:39.750
respectively.

00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:43.080
And lastly, you can
access Firestore directly

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:45.120
from the Cloud Console.

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:47.858
Many of you who come from
the Realtime Database world

00:10:47.858 --> 00:10:49.650
may know that you can
also access Firestore

00:10:49.650 --> 00:10:51.180
from the Firebase Console.

00:10:51.180 --> 00:10:53.522
And that's very common as well.

00:10:53.522 --> 00:10:55.230
One of the things to
note about these UIs

00:10:55.230 --> 00:10:57.820
is that they both operate
on the same projects.

00:10:57.820 --> 00:11:00.065
So if you create a project
in the Cloud Console,

00:11:00.065 --> 00:11:01.440
it's the same
project that you'll

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:04.080
see available in the
Firebase Console as well.

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:07.230
One of the benefits of using
the Google Cloud Console

00:11:07.230 --> 00:11:09.540
is that you have access
to all the other GCB

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:12.480
products that I mentioned,
including things like BigQuery.

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:13.380
And that's something
that we're actually

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:14.920
going to showcase
later in the demo

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:17.460
is how to get some
of the data that's

00:11:17.460 --> 00:11:22.050
in Firestore into BigQuery.

00:11:22.050 --> 00:11:26.820
So let's walk through a
couple of customer examples.

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:29.430
Since we went GA, we've seen
a huge diversity of workloads

00:11:29.430 --> 00:11:32.070
since launch, and some
of these were actually

00:11:32.070 --> 00:11:36.600
some of our early beta customers
as well back from 2017.

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:39.240
So we have a lot
of customers that

00:11:39.240 --> 00:11:41.350
span tons of
different industries,

00:11:41.350 --> 00:11:44.260
but they do have a couple
of things in common.

00:11:44.260 --> 00:11:46.950
The first is that a
lot of these companies

00:11:46.950 --> 00:11:49.450
needed a really
quick time to market.

00:11:49.450 --> 00:11:51.390
So they didn't want
to spend all this time

00:11:51.390 --> 00:11:54.510
provisioning servers,
picking things off the shelf,

00:11:54.510 --> 00:11:56.460
and stacking things together.

00:11:56.460 --> 00:11:57.960
Second is that they
needed something

00:11:57.960 --> 00:12:01.080
that would have the ability
to scale with their business.

00:12:01.080 --> 00:12:04.470
They wanted the same
solution for their 10 users

00:12:04.470 --> 00:12:06.630
that they want for
their 100,000, 1 million

00:12:06.630 --> 00:12:08.783
user use case.

00:12:08.783 --> 00:12:10.950
And the third thing is that
a lot of these companies

00:12:10.950 --> 00:12:13.020
had really lean
development teams,

00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:16.000
and they wanted to operate
in a serverless environment.

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:19.350
And as we know, Firestore
is a serverless environment.

00:12:19.350 --> 00:12:21.750
You don't have to
think in terms of nodes

00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:25.180
that you're provisioning or
capacity and resource planning.

00:12:25.180 --> 00:12:28.200
In fact, the pricing is actually
built on an operational model

00:12:28.200 --> 00:12:30.158
based on the reason rights
that you're actually

00:12:30.158 --> 00:12:31.710
doing against the systems.

00:12:31.710 --> 00:12:34.860
And this is huge, because
Firestore does not require

00:12:34.860 --> 00:12:36.453
a massive team to run it.

00:12:36.453 --> 00:12:37.620
And that's the beauty of it.

00:12:37.620 --> 00:12:39.600
The same team that
gets you started

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:42.300
can actually carry you
through the scaling

00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:45.100
of your application.

00:12:45.100 --> 00:12:47.970
The first one of these customers
that I want to highlight

00:12:47.970 --> 00:12:51.090
is a company called
Hawkin Dynamics.

00:12:51.090 --> 00:12:53.850
They build sophisticated
hardware and software

00:12:53.850 --> 00:12:56.100
for athletic
monitoring that's used

00:12:56.100 --> 00:12:59.200
by several professional
sports teams.

00:12:59.200 --> 00:13:01.085
So this hardware
that they develop

00:13:01.085 --> 00:13:02.460
includes what you
can see up here

00:13:02.460 --> 00:13:05.100
on the screen, which is
wireless force plates.

00:13:05.100 --> 00:13:08.850
And these wireless force plates
measure the impact and movement

00:13:08.850 --> 00:13:11.910
of athletes, and they
can track and measure

00:13:11.910 --> 00:13:13.290
the performance
of these athletes

00:13:13.290 --> 00:13:16.350
over time by capturing
this isometric data

00:13:16.350 --> 00:13:21.220
in their application and
sending it back too Firestore.

00:13:21.220 --> 00:13:23.622
Here's what their
architecture looks like.

00:13:23.622 --> 00:13:25.080
They have the
wireless force plates

00:13:25.080 --> 00:13:27.660
which capture and send the
data to the Hawkin Dynamics

00:13:27.660 --> 00:13:29.010
mobile app.

00:13:29.010 --> 00:13:33.570
This collects information across
athletes in many locations.

00:13:33.570 --> 00:13:36.240
And because they're testing
facilities are often

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:39.600
in low-connectivity areas,
as you can imagine-- fields,

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:42.340
maybe they're doing
something at high altitude--

00:13:42.340 --> 00:13:45.210
the ability to have
this offline sync

00:13:45.210 --> 00:13:47.130
is very, very important to them.

00:13:47.130 --> 00:13:49.230
So they can do their
tests, capture the data

00:13:49.230 --> 00:13:51.802
for the athletes, have it
sent back to the application,

00:13:51.802 --> 00:13:53.760
and then they have coaches
or other people that

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:56.250
are monitoring the athletic
statistics connecting

00:13:56.250 --> 00:14:00.150
from other devices like
tablets and desktop computers

00:14:00.150 --> 00:14:03.490
that can access this
information immediately.

00:14:03.490 --> 00:14:06.240
So this is very
important to them.

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:07.945
And they've actually
been kind enough

00:14:07.945 --> 00:14:09.070
to share their data models.

00:14:09.070 --> 00:14:11.445
So we can drill into a little
bit of how they've actually

00:14:11.445 --> 00:14:17.430
modeled their business using the
Cloud Firestore document model.

00:14:17.430 --> 00:14:21.510
So what you'll see up is a top
level collection called teams.

00:14:21.510 --> 00:14:25.330
Each of this collection
contains documents,

00:14:25.330 --> 00:14:28.230
one of each corresponding
to each of their teams.

00:14:28.230 --> 00:14:30.833
And these teams have
sub-collections.

00:14:30.833 --> 00:14:32.250
And these
sub-collections, you can

00:14:32.250 --> 00:14:33.458
see some of them listed here.

00:14:33.458 --> 00:14:35.370
It can include
settings, raw force

00:14:35.370 --> 00:14:38.920
data that's collected from the
force plates, different tests.

00:14:38.920 --> 00:14:41.363
If we drill in a level further,
they also a sub-collection

00:14:41.363 --> 00:14:42.030
called Athletes.

00:14:42.030 --> 00:14:44.607
So here there's a document
for every one of the athletes

00:14:44.607 --> 00:14:46.440
that they're collecting
this information on.

00:14:46.440 --> 00:14:48.420
And here they can drill
into all the tests

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:50.520
that have been
captured or performed

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:52.290
for that athlete over time.

00:14:55.320 --> 00:14:57.080
The next customer that
I want to highlight

00:14:57.080 --> 00:14:59.515
is one that you actually
may be familiar with.

00:14:59.515 --> 00:15:01.640
There's a high chance that
you went to this website

00:15:01.640 --> 00:15:03.710
because you're all
here today, or that you

00:15:03.710 --> 00:15:05.760
accessed the mobile app.

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:07.820
So our developer friends
over at the Nerdery--

00:15:07.820 --> 00:15:11.150
I think we have some of them
potentially in the audience.

00:15:11.150 --> 00:15:12.630
There.

00:15:12.630 --> 00:15:15.650
So they are a
digital consultancy

00:15:15.650 --> 00:15:19.310
that actually helped build the
conference web and mobile apps

00:15:19.310 --> 00:15:22.640
for us using Cloud Firestore.

00:15:22.640 --> 00:15:26.300
So they use Firestore
as an aggregation hub

00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:28.190
for all of the
conference data that's

00:15:28.190 --> 00:15:33.360
serving the session information,
the breakouts, et cetera.

00:15:33.360 --> 00:15:35.180
And again, we actually
have a data model

00:15:35.180 --> 00:15:37.470
to show you and
see how this works.

00:15:37.470 --> 00:15:40.460
So here you can see
an actual document

00:15:40.460 --> 00:15:44.480
that represents content that's
being served to the next home

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:45.360
page.

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:49.790
And in this case, it's
the mobile app page.

00:15:49.790 --> 00:15:52.440
So you can see the
content being served here.

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:56.070
It also has static content
that's being stored elsewhere.

00:15:56.070 --> 00:15:58.760
And you can see the path
to that information here.

00:15:58.760 --> 00:16:00.590
If I click to the
next, this is actually

00:16:00.590 --> 00:16:03.440
a high level collection
representing all

00:16:03.440 --> 00:16:04.635
of the different next event.

00:16:04.635 --> 00:16:06.260
So the one that's
highlighted right now

00:16:06.260 --> 00:16:10.280
is the one that you're in here
today for, which is Next SF.

00:16:10.280 --> 00:16:12.920
You'll also see entries here
for the future next event

00:16:12.920 --> 00:16:15.260
that we have in
Tokyo and London.

00:16:18.070 --> 00:16:20.960
And here is actually the
session we're in right now.

00:16:20.960 --> 00:16:25.660
So this document
represents the session ID.

00:16:25.660 --> 00:16:28.000
You can see here
we have information

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:30.682
about the session type, the
different related fields--

00:16:30.682 --> 00:16:32.140
so these are sessions
it recommends

00:16:32.140 --> 00:16:34.420
you go to if you also
have gone to this one--

00:16:34.420 --> 00:16:37.995
and then speaker ID, which
refer to both Ben and myself.

00:16:37.995 --> 00:16:40.120
Something that's not on
the screen that also exists

00:16:40.120 --> 00:16:43.420
in the app is a
field lower down that

00:16:43.420 --> 00:16:45.955
tells the capacity of
each of the sessions.

00:16:45.955 --> 00:16:48.360
So you can imagine if
the capacity is full,

00:16:48.360 --> 00:16:52.510
it will no longer let
someone register for that.

00:16:52.510 --> 00:16:56.800
So when you first visit the
conference site, what you're

00:16:56.800 --> 00:16:59.170
seeing is a session
and speaker information

00:16:59.170 --> 00:17:01.450
that's being loaded
from Firestore,

00:17:01.450 --> 00:17:04.930
as well as some of these static
images and assets from Google

00:17:04.930 --> 00:17:07.300
Cloud Storage.

00:17:07.300 --> 00:17:09.640
When you sign in with
either your Google account

00:17:09.640 --> 00:17:11.650
or personal email
address, you're

00:17:11.650 --> 00:17:13.720
actually hitting
Firebase Auth which

00:17:13.720 --> 00:17:16.690
is authenticating you
and making sure you have

00:17:16.690 --> 00:17:19.119
the proper account and
you have access to all

00:17:19.119 --> 00:17:21.190
the information you're seeing.

00:17:21.190 --> 00:17:24.099
Then when you register for
a session like this one

00:17:24.099 --> 00:17:27.790
that you're in today, the
app makes an API call out,

00:17:27.790 --> 00:17:30.850
it updates the
information in Firestore,

00:17:30.850 --> 00:17:35.550
and immediately updates that
information to the user.

00:17:35.550 --> 00:17:39.390
So next what I want to
do is invite Ben onstage.

00:17:39.390 --> 00:17:41.790
Now that you've seen
some customer examples,

00:17:41.790 --> 00:17:43.360
I want him to
actually demonstrate

00:17:43.360 --> 00:17:46.088
Firestore for you in action.

00:17:46.088 --> 00:17:46.880
Thank you, Natalie.

