00:18:51.020 --> 00:18:52.880
So in Firestore, we
actually have a document

00:18:52.880 --> 00:18:54.650
for every single pixel
that's maintaining

00:18:54.650 --> 00:18:58.100
the color of that pixel,
the last updated timestamp,

00:18:58.100 --> 00:19:00.620
as well as a
partition that we're

00:19:00.620 --> 00:19:04.970
using to parallelize the
event propagating to the UI

00:19:04.970 --> 00:19:07.820
that we'll be able to see in
the real-time demo in just

00:19:07.820 --> 00:19:09.900
a few minutes.

00:19:09.900 --> 00:19:12.920
So given that there are the
four quadrants, each of them

00:19:12.920 --> 00:19:17.510
have 10,000, this can handle
up to 40,000 concurrent users,

00:19:17.510 --> 00:19:19.550
essentially kind of
poking at the bits

00:19:19.550 --> 00:19:20.940
to represent each of the pixels.

00:19:20.940 --> 00:19:22.340
So we've got a
simulation service

00:19:22.340 --> 00:19:24.770
running on GKE that's
actually running

00:19:24.770 --> 00:19:27.380
a simulation of
some user activity,

00:19:27.380 --> 00:19:29.630
so you will be seeing
pixels kind of updating

00:19:29.630 --> 00:19:32.070
when we get over to the demo.

00:19:32.070 --> 00:19:34.770
So we've got this
application up and running.

00:19:34.770 --> 00:19:37.100
We've gained a fair
amount of user traction.

00:19:37.100 --> 00:19:39.440
And the business comes
