00:22:26.370 --> 00:22:27.870
So it's very responsive.

00:22:27.870 --> 00:22:31.480
It can give you a great
experience for your users.

00:22:31.480 --> 00:22:34.950
And we'll be able to see
that changing over time

00:22:34.950 --> 00:22:36.983
during the rest of
the presentation.

00:22:36.983 --> 00:22:38.400
So first, we're
going to jump over

00:22:38.400 --> 00:22:42.150
to Cloud Firestore in
the Google Console,

00:22:42.150 --> 00:22:45.720
and we're going to take a look
into the data model that's

00:22:45.720 --> 00:22:48.610
actually backing the Firepixels
app right now on the left hand

00:22:48.610 --> 00:22:49.110
side.

00:22:52.230 --> 00:22:54.210
So here on the left
hand column, we've

00:22:54.210 --> 00:22:56.910
got three different collections,
top level collections.

00:22:56.910 --> 00:23:00.570
One for devices, one for
input, and one for pixels.

00:23:00.570 --> 00:23:02.650
The collection for devices--

00:23:02.650 --> 00:23:05.980
this is actually a
heartbeat collection.

00:23:05.980 --> 00:23:08.190
So for each of the
respective clients that

00:23:08.190 --> 00:23:09.960
are managing each
of those pixels

00:23:09.960 --> 00:23:12.030
as part of the simulation,
we're essentially

00:23:12.030 --> 00:23:14.880
tracking the heartbeat of the
last time one of those users

00:23:14.880 --> 00:23:17.250
actually did something for us.

00:23:17.250 --> 00:23:20.250
If we move down to
the inputs collection,

00:23:20.250 --> 00:23:21.690
we can see a 0 through 3.

00:23:21.690 --> 00:23:25.020
The 0 through 3 here represent
each of the colored quadrants

00:23:25.020 --> 00:23:28.030
over on the left hand side,
starting top to bottom,

00:23:28.030 --> 00:23:29.970
left to right.

00:23:29.970 --> 00:23:30.480
Excuse me.

00:23:30.480 --> 00:23:31.780
Left to right, top to bottom.

00:23:31.780 --> 00:23:34.380
So 0, 1, 2, 3.

00:23:34.380 --> 00:23:36.810
In each of those documents,
we can see the color.

00:23:36.810 --> 00:23:39.420
We can also see the updated
timestamp of the last time

00:23:39.420 --> 00:23:41.220
that quadrant was updated.

00:23:41.220 --> 00:23:43.410
And if we look at the
pixels collection,

00:23:43.410 --> 00:23:47.280
here we can see a list
of the 40,000 documents

00:23:47.280 --> 00:23:49.310
that represent
each of the pixels.

00:23:49.310 --> 00:23:51.990
So the name of the document
that we've got here

00:23:51.990 --> 00:23:54.960
is essentially 3
digits dash 3 digits.

00:23:54.960 --> 00:23:58.950
This is representing the
xy coordinate in the grid.

00:23:58.950 --> 00:24:02.790
And we can see the color
for that particular pixel,

00:24:02.790 --> 00:24:05.130
which partition that
pixel is associated with,

00:24:05.130 --> 00:24:07.770
and the last updated timestamp.

00:24:07.770 --> 00:24:11.030
So while the
simulation is running--

00:24:11.030 --> 00:24:12.030
wow, we got it all blue.

00:24:12.030 --> 00:24:15.220
That's really unusual--
let's take the top right

00:24:15.220 --> 00:24:18.790
and let's change
this to be yellow.

00:24:18.790 --> 00:24:19.622
So I can hit that.

00:24:19.622 --> 00:24:21.080
That's going to
trigger the events.

00:24:21.080 --> 00:24:23.272
All of the event
