00:36:57.063 --> 00:36:58.480
So this is really
nice if you need

00:36:58.480 --> 00:37:01.900
to put it on a display
in your bullpen,

00:37:01.900 --> 00:37:05.438
or if you only want to share
it with maybe your manager

00:37:05.438 --> 00:37:07.480
before it's published to
the rest of the company,

00:37:07.480 --> 00:37:09.522
you've got full ACL control
the same way that you

00:37:09.522 --> 00:37:11.650
do with Google Docs.

00:37:11.650 --> 00:37:14.770
So here on the top row, we
can see three different charts

00:37:14.770 --> 00:37:16.040
with color breakdowns.

00:37:16.040 --> 00:37:19.990
So these color breakdowns
are answering the questions

00:37:19.990 --> 00:37:22.870
that we mentioned at the
beginning of the demo

00:37:22.870 --> 00:37:24.680
around the business questions.

00:37:24.680 --> 00:37:28.870
So the business question
is essentially, how many--

00:37:28.870 --> 00:37:31.270
or, excuse me--
for the top row is,

00:37:31.270 --> 00:37:35.260
what is the ratio of a
particular color per time

00:37:35.260 --> 00:37:36.890
bucket over a period of time?

00:37:36.890 --> 00:37:42.550
So here we're saying for the
last 30 minutes, about 63%

00:37:42.550 --> 00:37:46.960
of the color changes were to
blue, and the other 37% or so

00:37:46.960 --> 00:37:48.040
were to yellow.

00:37:48.040 --> 00:37:50.760
So this makes it really
easy to look at the chart.

00:37:50.760 --> 00:37:53.260
And having some context around
the way that the app behaves,

00:37:53.260 --> 00:37:54.430
you can just kind of
look at it and get

00:37:54.430 --> 00:37:56.055
an idea of what the
app probably looked

00:37:56.055 --> 00:37:58.160
like at that point in time.

00:37:58.160 --> 00:38:00.800
And then that expands
to two days ago,

00:38:00.800 --> 00:38:03.640
as well as over the past week.

00:38:03.640 --> 00:38:07.120
In the bottom left
we've got our line chart

00:38:07.120 --> 00:38:09.730
that's showing the
number of distinct colors

00:38:09.730 --> 00:38:12.490
that were changed
to in that frame.

00:38:12.490 --> 00:38:16.060
So here we can see that we
had two that we changed to.

00:38:16.060 --> 00:38:17.830
One time we only had one.

00:38:17.830 --> 00:38:21.110
One time we actually got
all the way up to three.

00:38:21.110 --> 00:38:23.930
You know, just various
interesting things.

00:38:23.930 --> 00:38:26.650
And then the center chart is
showing our ingestion latency.

00:38:26.650 --> 00:38:30.100
So this is essentially
showing in seconds

00:38:30.100 --> 00:38:32.380
how long it's taking
from the document being

00:38:32.380 --> 00:38:35.895
written into Firestore for
us to write it into BigQuery.

00:38:35.895 --> 00:38:37.270
So this is
essentially showing us

00:38:37.270 --> 00:38:40.850
an end-to-end latency of getting
data into our data warehouse.

00:38:40.850 --> 00:38:45.290
So the vast majority are being
handled in less than 2 seconds.

00:38:45.290 --> 00:38:48.910
And we've got a little
bit of plateau here.

00:38:48.910 --> 00:38:51.460
This is actually I
believe due to the fact

00:38:51.460 --> 00:38:53.770
that the Cloud
Functions are spinning

00:38:53.770 --> 00:38:57.730
back up after being
spun down between stages

00:38:57.730 --> 00:38:59.950
of the simulation running.

00:38:59.950 --> 00:39:02.530
And then there's almost a
full drop off by the time

00:39:02.530 --> 00:39:03.860
we reach 7 seconds.

00:39:03.860 --> 00:39:06.760
So the vast, vast majority
of all of our events

00:39:06.760 --> 00:39:09.970
are being put into our data
warehouse in 8 seconds or so.

00:39:09.970 --> 00:39:13.620
[MUSIC PLAYING]

